    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d156">

                <pb facs="0294" n="195" sameAs="frus:frus1936v05/pg195"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">835.6363/462: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Argentina</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Weddell</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Buenos
                                Aires</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1936-09-22T17:00:00-04:00">September 22, 1936—5
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 5:37 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>195. Department’s 124, September 21, 7 p.m. Standard Oil Company informs me that
                    Harden and Bronson,<anchor n="29" xml:id="d156-f1"/> now here discussing
                    situation at length with Argentine authorities, have sent them a joint letter
                    containing main objections to marketing arrangement. Standard Oil Company states
                    that idea of time limit to accept or reject arrangement has apparently been
                    abandoned for the present.</p>
                <p>Will keep Department informed by telegraph.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Weddell</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d156-f1" n="29" xml:id="d156-f1-content">
                <p>In following telegrams appears as Brousson, who was one of the directors of the
                    Dutch Shell-Mex Co.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
